# Testing guidance

- New or changed logic needs a test for the happy path and at least one realistic
  failure case — not just the case that was easiest to make pass.
- Never fake data, mock away the behavior under test, or weaken an assertion just to
  make a test pass. If a test is wrong, fix the test; if the code is wrong, fix the code.
- Re-run the full affected test file after every change, not just the new test — a
  passing new test next to a broken old one is a regression, not a success.
- A flaky test earns a more reliable reproduction, not a longer timeout and a shrug.
